PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: A solid, fair set. Every level is unique by line logic and has a 0% trap rate. Each chapter is built around one named idea, and the finales, Masterworks and breathers sit in the right slots. The biggest weakness is sameness. Most levels follow one script: find one exact-fit line, then chain outward. The "aha" is often stated in the intent but is not much more than a standard overlap or reach. Observatory "deep-chain" is not a mechanic, only normal solving, and its levels read as filler. Several second-half chapters (Kiln 3-5, Observatory 4-5, Grand Exhibit 3-5, Lantern 9-10) open with practise levels that play like finales (casual 53-60%). Several pictures (Anchor, Deer, Horse Statue, Orrery) are hard to read as their subject, which weakens the reveal. Late-third averages meet the casual ≤65% target, and no level is unfair. Curve: Chapters 1-2 (Shore, Woods) ramp cleanly: the order is teach, practise, twist, boss, breather, with the right ideas in sequence (exact fit, then overlap, then reach). Colour (Market), voids (Hall) and stamps (Mosaic) each get two clean teach levels before a combine. The weak points are in the second half. (1) Chapters 8-10 chain smudged, cracked and stamp onto colour-cross, and several levels there start at a hardness that belongs to a finale (L81-82, L87-88, L99-100, L111-113 at 53-60% casual). Practise levels should land around 70-80% and leave the 50s to bosses and finales. (2) "Deep-chain" (Observatory) is a label, not a new rule, so the chapter has no clear teaching spine. Give it a real hook, such as an X-wall chain or long forced sequences. (3) Breathers are well placed (L7, 19, 31, 43, 55, 67, 79, 91, 103, 115), but many are exact-fit rows plus a chain, so they feel identical across chapters. (4) Board-wide: many bosses and finales win by the same first move, "row N exact fit, chain outward". Vary the opener with a column-first start, a no-full-line start, or a late-chain level. (5) The late-third casual average is about 60%, which meets the target, and the skilled range is 80-99%.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L81 (6/10): 56% casual against 76% neighbour: stamp + smudge + colour is introduced at once; the hidden-cell stamp is the only help. → Add a second stamp in column 3 or raise anchors; target about 70%.
L82 (6/10): 56% casual, 13x11 with the hidden row touching edges; a spike for a 10th-position level and a two-in-a-row hard stretch. → Make the edge columns exact fits and state the sky more clearly.
L87 (6/10): 66% casual, 5 passes; a steep jump from 82% for the first colour+cracked. → Remove the chip at row 8 and resolve the ? by one wall only.
L88 (6/10): 54% casual, 87% skilled; the cracked ? needs a trunk-based deduction that is long. → Add a second, more direct bound or shrink it.
L95 (6/10): A noodle bowl whose reveal is unclear; 57% casual on a combine where bounds rely on 'no gold' lookups. → Re-draw chopsticks as a clear shape and add a direct bound.
L97 (6/10): 'Deep-chain' is not a rule; the level is normal overlap, so the chapter's teach has no new idea. → Teach a defined idea, such as an X-wall splitting a line, with one clean example.
L99 (6/10): 63% casual after 83% — a spike for an early practise level; the wizard hat is sparse. → Make row 8 exact and fill the hat with stars; target 75%.
L100 (6/10): 55% casual for a 4th-of-12 practise level; crescent is hard. → Shrink to 10x8 and add a second anchor.
L104 (6/10): 53% casual / 83% skilled on a practise; the astronaut is complex and the glint/boots gaps feel fiddly. → Remove one of the gaps and let the spine do more work.
L111 (6/10): 56% casual on a 3rd-of-12 practise; the vase is complex. → Simplify the bloom.
L112 (6/10): 54% casual on a 4th-of-12 practise, 3 colours and cracked. → Reduce to two colours or add a stamp.
L117 (6/10): 82% skilled, 61% casual; the cracked ? is trivial (2) while the wing colour cross is heavy. → Make the ? span something longer.
L3 (7/10): Only 5x6 and the picture is hard to read as a jellyfish; very short. → Add tentacle detail (a 7x8 canvas) so the reveal reads and overlap is used twice.
L5 (7/10): Twist is the same overlap as L3 with a chain on top; little new. → Make the 4 in row 5 solvable only after a column X-wall, so X's feel like the aha.
L7 (7/10): A breather straight after a boss is correct, but it is another exact-fit row, nearly a repeat of L1/L2. → Open with a column and swap the picture for a more charming one.
L9 (7/10): The anchor is hard to recognise, and the arms are guessed from one-by-one reading. → Redraw with a clearer ring and stock so the silhouette reads, and keep the arms deducible.
L10 (7/10): Same shape of insight as L9 (exact row then X's pin neighbours). → Start with the column overlap instead so the flipper row is the payoff.
L14 (7/10): Teaches two things (fill, reach) and no mirror; adjacent to another 89-92% level. → Make it a pure reach lesson and drop the full row.
L15 (7/10): Row 7's overlap is the entry; reach is secondary. → Make the perch row 8/9 so the reach is the only way in.
L16 (7/10): Hedgehog is mostly a solid blob; clutter-free but forgettable. → Add spines gaps (paper in row 3) so the legs matter more.
L17 (7/10): Row 9 full and col 3 full give two free starts, so the twist is mild. → Remove one anchor (make row 9 an 10 in 11) so reach has to carry.
L18 (7/10): Deer silhouette is unreadable in the reveal; 12x12 with large symmetric bottom is padding. → Reshape the lower half into legs/neck and break the mirror to cut busywork.
L19 (7/10): Another exact-fit row breather; at 14 moves it is fine but familiar. → Keep it, but start from a column fit to differ from L7.
L21 (7/10): Column 10 gives the whole thing away on pass 1. → Shift the 1 8 so it has one spare cell, forcing a cross-check.
L22 (7/10): 34 moves is long and the tail curl is mostly repeated reach with little new insight. → Trim the size to 10x10 or add one clear gap that requires a different deduction.

